BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.2 Tm (1. A severe rise in intracranial pressure produces systemic hypertension with reflex bradycardia. What is) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 561.5 Tm (this response?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 544.8 Tm (Answer: D. Cushing reflex.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 528.5 Tm (Explanation: The Cushing response reflects brainstem ischaemia driving systemic hypertension, with) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 515.4 Tm (baroreflex-mediated bradycardia. Brain perfusion is threatened as raised intracranial pressure compresses cerebral) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 502.3 Tm (vessels.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 486.2 Tm (Not A: Pulmonary hypoxic vasoconstriction is a local lung response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 472.8 Tm (Not B: Atrial stretch does not explain raised ICP-driven hypertension.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 459.4 Tm (Not C: Bainbridge responds to increased atrial filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 446.0 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q17 | 22-23) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 422.8 Tm (2. Which pressure relationship helps prevent fluid accumulation in normal pulmonary alveoli?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 406.1 Tm (Answer: C. Relatively low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 389.8 Tm (Explanation: Low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure relative to plasma oncotic forces helps minimise) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 376.7 Tm (filtration into air spaces.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 360.6 Tm (Not A: Pulmonary capillary pressure is much lower than systemic arterial pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 347.2 Tm (Not B: Plasma proteins contribute the inward oncotic force.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 333.8 Tm (Not D: Alveolar spaces normally contain little free fluid and protein.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 320.4 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q32 | 31) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 297.2 Tm (3. A patient in compensated haemorrhagic shock has cold, pale skin. Which mechanism accounts for this?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 280.5 Tm (Answer: A. Increased sympathetic cutaneous vasoconstriction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.2 Tm (Explanation: Sympathetic constriction reduces skin flow to preserve perfusion for vital organs, producing pallor) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.1 Tm (and coolness.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 235.0 Tm (Not B: Loss of sympathetic tone would usually warm and flush skin.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 221.6 Tm (Not C: Higher cutaneous blood volume tends to increase redness.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 208.2 Tm (Not D: The primary response is systemic compensation, not skin warming.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 194.8 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q48 | 39, 41) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 171.6 Tm (4. A cuff that is far too small is used on a large upper arm. Which measurement error is likely?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 154.9 Tm (Answer: B. An undersized cuff gives a falsely high reading.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 138.6 Tm (Explanation: An undersized cuff transmits pressure poorly around a large arm and commonly overestimates measured) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.5 Tm (pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 109.4 Tm (Not A: Cuff size materially affects readings.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 96.0 Tm (Not C: Both systolic and diastolic measurements may be distorted.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 82.6 Tm (Not D: A small cuff usually overestimates rather than underestimates BP.) Tj ET
