BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 02 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q64 | 79-80; Chapter 10 ?8) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 721.8 Tm (5. The CSF volume is about 150 mL and production is 550 mL/day. Approximately how many complete turnovers) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 708.1 Tm (occur per day?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 691.4 Tm (Answer: A. About 4.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 675.1 Tm (Explanation: Dividing daily production by total CSF volume gives 550/150 ~ 3.7 turnovers each day.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 659.0 Tm (Not B: Two turnovers would require about 300 mL/day.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 645.6 Tm (Not C: One turnover would represent only about 150 mL/day.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 632.2 Tm (Not D: Six turnovers would correspond to around 900 mL/day.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 618.8 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q8 | 14) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 595.6 Tm (6. Which recording satisfies the basic chapter criteria for a normal sinus rhythm at rest?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 578.9 Tm (Answer: A. Rate 72/min, equal R-R gaps, identical preceding P waves, PR 0.16 s.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 562.6 Tm (Explanation: Normal sinus rhythm at rest requires an appropriate rate, regular R-R intervals, a consistent P before) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 549.5 Tm (each QRS, and normal conduction intervals; the listed 0.16-s PR satisfies this.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 533.4 Tm (Not B: Without a visible P wave before each QRS, sinus origin is not established.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 520.0 Tm (Not C: Unequal R-R intervals do not satisfy the chapter's basic regularity criterion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 506.6 Tm (Not D: A PR of 0.26 s exceeds the chapter's stated 0.12-0.20-s range.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.2 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q47 | Chapter 6 ?8 \(supplementary\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 470.0 Tm (7. Increasing capillary hydrostatic pressure while other Starling forces stay unchanged tends to produce) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 456.3 Tm (what?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 439.6 Tm (Answer: B. More fluid filtration into tissues.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 423.3 Tm (Explanation: A higher capillary hydrostatic force favours filtration across the microvascular wall toward tissue.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 410.2 Tm (Raising venous pressure is one way to produce this hydrostatic change.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 394.1 Tm (Not A: The pressure favours outward, not reduced, filtration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 380.7 Tm (Not C: Lymphatic flow generally responds to excess tissue fluid rather than reversing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 367.3 Tm (Not D: Plasma oncotic pressure does not automatically increase.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 353.9 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q82 | 85-88) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 330.7 Tm (8. A patient develops cardiac tamponade. Why can stroke volume fall despite a normal blood volume?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 314.0 Tm (Answer: D. High pericardial pressure restricts ventricular filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 297.7 Tm (Explanation: Elevated intrapericardial pressure limits end-diastolic filling and preload, reducing stroke volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 284.6 Tm (The restricted ventricular volume prevents an adequate Frank-Starling response.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 268.5 Tm (Not A: Tamponade reduces the ability of ventricles to expand.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 255.1 Tm (Not B: Venous return and effective filling become impaired.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 241.7 Tm (Not C: Valvular opening depends on pressure, but reduced preload is the central problem.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.3 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q38 | 43-44; Chapter 7 ?6) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 205.1 Tm (9. Why are superficial neck veins often less distended in a healthy person who stands upright?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 188.4 Tm (Answer: A. Venous pressure decreases above the heart.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 172.1 Tm (Explanation: Gravity lowers venous pressure above the heart; collapsible superficial veins can flatten at low) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 159.0 Tm (transmural pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 142.9 Tm (Not B: Venous return does not normally reverse direction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 129.5 Tm (Not C: Systemic venous pressure remains far below aortic systolic pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 116.1 Tm (Not D: Arterial flow does not open venous valves in the neck.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 102.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q78 | 78, 97-98; Chapter 12 ?7) Tj ET
