BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 02 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (10. Arterial pressure inside an arteriole is unchanged, but pressure in its surrounding tissue rises. What) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (happens to transmural pressure?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: A. It decreases because the pressure difference narrows.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Transmural pressure equals internal pressure minus surrounding tissue pressure; increasing the latter) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (reduces the difference.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not B: A higher external pressure reduces, rather than widens, the difference.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not C: It equals internal pressure only when external pressure is zero.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: Transmural pressure depends on both internal and external pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q6 | 8-10) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (11. A severely underperfused patient begins breathing rapidly, although the lungs were initially healthy.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.0 Tm (What drives this change?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.3 Tm (Answer: C. Lactic acidosis stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.0 Tm (Explanation: Inadequate tissue oxygen delivery promotes anaerobic metabolism and lactic acidosis. The resulting) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 545.9 Tm (acidemia can stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors and increase ventilation; reduced oxygen delivery alone is not) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 532.8 Tm (equivalent to low arterial oxygen tension.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not A: Aerobic metabolism is limited by inadequate oxygen delivery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Not B: Chemoreceptor stimulation, rather than suppression, promotes respiration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 489.9 Tm (Not D: Excess oxygen is not the initiating problem in shock.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 476.5 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q25 | Circulation 3, slides 23) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 453.3 Tm (12. Why may advanced liver disease cause generalised oedema even without venous obstruction?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 436.6 Tm (Answer: B. Less albumin synthesis reduces plasma oncotic pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (Explanation: Reduced albumin production lowers intravascular colloid oncotic pressure, allowing more net) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 407.2 Tm (filtration and oedema.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not A: Advanced liver disease commonly reduces albumin synthesis.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Not C: Plasma protein concentration is decreased, not increased.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.3 Tm (Not D: Lymphatic valve closure is not the universal mechanism.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 350.9 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q87 | 93) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.7 Tm (13. How does local hypoxia affect most systemic arterioles versus pulmonary arterioles?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 311.0 Tm (Answer: D. Systemic vessels dilate; pulmonary vessels constrict.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.7 Tm (Explanation: Systemic hypoxic tissues commonly dilate to improve supply, whereas low alveolar oxygen constricts) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.6 Tm (adjacent pulmonary arterioles to improve ventilation-perfusion matching.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.5 Tm (Not A: The response directions have been reversed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.1 Tm (Not B: Systemic tissues usually need increased flow in hypoxia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.7 Tm (Not C: Oxygen tension influences both beds, but their dominant local responses differ.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 225.3 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q37 | 32; Chapter 24 ?5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 202.1 Tm (14. A pressure tracing shows atrial contraction shortly after an ECG P wave and ventricular contraction) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 188.4 Tm (shortly after QRS. What principle explains both delays?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 171.7 Tm (Answer: A. Electrical excitation normally precedes the mechanical response of cardiac muscle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 155.4 Tm (Explanation: The electrical depolarisation that activates cardiac muscle precedes its force development. P) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 142.3 Tm (precedes atrial systole, and QRS precedes ventricular systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 126.2 Tm (Not B: This reverses the normal electrical-to-mechanical sequence.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.8 Tm (Not C: The T wave reflects ventricular electrical recovery, after ventricular depolarisation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 99.4 Tm (Not D: Pressure changes follow electrical activation and are not what skin ECG leads measure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 86.0 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q34 | 6-11; Chapter 6 ?5) Tj ET
