q .08 .14 .26 rg 0 792 595 50 re f Q
BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 02 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (15. A student faints after receiving distressing news, with a slow pulse and low arterial pressure. Which) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (mechanism best matches the lecture?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: B. Emotional inhibition of pressor activity with vagal predominance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Fear or grief can favour a depressor, vagal response with bradycardia, vasodilation and hypotension.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.2 Tm (Not A: Cushing physiology produces hypertension with reflex bradycardia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 668.8 Tm (Not C: beta1 stimulation would generally raise the pulse and contractility.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.4 Tm (Not D: Overfilled atria may produce a faster pulse in the described reflex.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.0 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q59 | 30, 33-34) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 618.8 Tm (16. What extra perspective do the chest leads V1-V6 add to the standard limb-lead views?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.1 Tm (Answer: D. They show cardiac electrical vectors in the horizontal plane.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 585.8 Tm (Explanation: The limb leads provide frontal-plane views, while the precordial leads examine the same heart from) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (different points around the horizontal plane.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 556.6 Tm (Not A: Chest leads, like limb leads, still record surface electrical potentials.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.2 Tm (Not B: Frontal and horizontal views complement rather than replace one another.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not C: Both sets of leads record combined cardiac electrical events.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q10 | 3-5; Chapter 6 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.2 Tm (17. During progressive shock, capillary hydrostatic pressure paradoxically rises while systemic arterial) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 479.5 Tm (pressure is falling. Which microvascular pattern best explains it?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 462.8 Tm (Answer: C. Open capillary entry with constricted venules.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 446.5 Tm (Explanation: With capillary entry relatively open but venular outflow restricted, blood stagnates and local) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (capillary pressure rises, driving fluid into the interstitium.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.3 Tm (Not A: Lower entry with free outflow would not cause the described stasis.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 403.9 Tm (Not B: Greater drainage would reduce rather than increase local stagnation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.5 Tm (Not D: Both reduced entry and improved drainage favour lower capillary pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.1 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q37 | Circulation 3, slide 26; Chapter 20 ?5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 353.9 Tm (18. A stiff ventricle has high filling pressure but a preserved ejection fraction. What is the primary) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.2 Tm (defect?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 323.5 Tm (Answer: A. Impaired relaxation and filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 307.2 Tm (Explanation: Diastolic failure reflects impaired relaxation and stiff ventricular filling; the fraction of blood) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.1 Tm (ejected can remain normal despite elevated filling pressures and reduced filling volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.0 Tm (Not B: Increased contractility is not the primary defect.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.6 Tm (Not C: SA-node dysfunction does not define diastolic heart failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.2 Tm (Not D: A stiff ventricle has reduced, not excessive, compliance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 237.8 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q49 | Chapter 21 ?2 \(supplementary\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 214.6 Tm (19. Lead II has an upright QRS while aVR has a downward QRS during the same beat. What best explains the) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 200.9 Tm (difference?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 184.2 Tm (Answer: A. Each lead projects the same electrical vector onto a different axis.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 167.9 Tm (Explanation: A lead is a directional electrical view. The normal mean ventricular depolarisation vector points) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 154.8 Tm (generally towards Lead II and away from aVR.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 138.7 Tm (Not B: Both QRS complexes represent ventricular depolarisation, despite opposite polarity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.3 Tm (Not C: Both leads sample the electrical activity of the same heartbeat.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 111.9 Tm (Not D: Lead orientation does not change the sequence of atrial and ventricular activation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 98.5 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q4 | 3-5; Chapter 6 ?2) Tj ET
