BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 02 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (25. A region of heart muscle starts working harder. Which local product of its own metabolism helps increase) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (its blood flow?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: D. Adenosine formed as ATP is used up.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Working muscle uses ATP faster, and its breakdown products \(adenosine, ADP, AMP\) build up and dilate) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (the local arterioles. Flow rises in proportion to how hard the tissue works, which is why the heart relies so heavily) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 672.1 Tm (on this.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not A: Platelet serotonin constricts vessels, and it comes from platelets, not from the working muscle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Not B: Angiotensin II is a circulating constrictor, not a product of the muscle's metabolism.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 629.2 Tm (Not C: Noradrenaline is a nerve transmitter that mainly constricts arterioles via alpha1 receptors.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 615.8 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q9 | 6) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.6 Tm (26. What physiological purpose is served by slow conduction through the AV node?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.9 Tm (Answer: D. It allows atrial contraction to finish ventricular filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (Explanation: The AV delay separates atrial and ventricular activation so atrial systole can augment ventricular) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 546.5 Tm (filling before ventricular systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.4 Tm (Not A: Normal impulses subsequently travel through His and Purkinje fibres.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.0 Tm (Not B: The sequence is atrial activation before ventricular activation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.6 Tm (Not C: The aortic valve remains closed during ordinary atrial systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 490.2 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q14 | 8-10; Chapter 3 ?5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 467.0 Tm (27. Why is a patient with significant coronary narrowing more vulnerable to angina when heart rate rises) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 453.3 Tm (sharply?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 436.6 Tm (Answer: D. Shorter diastole and higher myocardial demand.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (Explanation: Tachycardia increases cardiac oxygen demand and shortens diastole, reducing the time available for) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 407.2 Tm (left ventricular coronary perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not A: An absent P wave is not the explanation of demand-related angina.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Not B: Myocardial work and oxygen consumption generally rise with tachycardia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.3 Tm (Not C: The primary limitation is coronary supply through stenosed arteries during shortened diastole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 350.9 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q26 | 27-29; Chapter 23 ?5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.7 Tm (28. Which statement best reflects the updated interpretation of capillary fluid balance?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 311.0 Tm (Answer: A. The glycocalyx regulates filtration; lymph returns excess fluid.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.7 Tm (Explanation: The revised Starling model includes the glycocalyx and emphasises that sustained net filtration is) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.6 Tm (normally cleared by lymphatics.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.5 Tm (Not B: Lymphatic return remains vital even at normal pressures.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.1 Tm (Not C: The classical arterial filtration-venous reabsorption picture overstates sustained reabsorption.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.7 Tm (Not D: Protein distributions can influence effective oncotic gradients.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 225.3 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q84 | 85-88; Chapter 13 ?6 \(modern clarification\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 202.1 Tm (29. Minutes after a severe allergic exposure, a patient's pressure drops with generalised swelling. Which) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 188.4 Tm (vascular action is most relevant?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 171.7 Tm (Answer: A. Histamine-related dilatation with increased capillary permeability.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 155.4 Tm (Explanation: Anaphylaxis releases mediators including histamine that widen vessels and increase capillary leakage,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 142.3 Tm (reducing effective perfusion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 126.2 Tm (Not B: Anaphylaxis typically causes dilation and increased leak.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.8 Tm (Not C: Higher pump performance would not explain the sudden widespread leakage.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 99.4 Tm (Not D: Occult bleeding is not the primary allergic vascular mechanism.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 86.0 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q46 | Circulation 3, slides 29) Tj ET
