BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (40. A ventricle has an end-diastolic volume of 130 mL and an end-systolic volume of 55 mL. What is its stroke) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (volume?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: B. 75 mL.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Stroke volume is EDV minus ESV, so 130 - 55 = 75 mL per beat.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.2 Tm (Not A: One hundred thirty mL is end-diastolic volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 668.8 Tm (Not C: Fifty-five mL is end-systolic volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.4 Tm (Not D: Adding EDV and ESV does not give stroke volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.0 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q22 | 16; Chapter 4 ?7) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 618.8 Tm (41. How does CSF substantially reduce the effective weight of the brain?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.1 Tm (Answer: B. It provides buoyant support against gravity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 585.8 Tm (Explanation: Immersion provides buoyancy, reducing the brain's effective supported weight from roughly 1400-1500 g) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (to around 50 g.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 556.6 Tm (Not A: The tissue density does not increase to create buoyancy.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.2 Tm (Not C: Brain blood volume remains substantial.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not D: Lower oxygen consumption does not explain mechanical support.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q9 | 13-15) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.2 Tm (42. In a whole-body Fick measurement, which blood should the return-side oxygen sample represent?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 476.5 Tm (Answer: B. Mixed venous blood entering the lungs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 460.2 Tm (Explanation: A mixed venous sample from pulmonary artery reflects oxygen after extraction across the whole body.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 444.1 Tm (Not A: Arterial blood provides the other concentration in the equation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 430.7 Tm (Not C: One local vein does not represent average whole-body extraction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.3 Tm (Not D: Active muscle has unusually high local extraction and can misrepresent the body.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 403.9 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q46 | 52-53; Chapter 8 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 380.7 Tm (43. Why is 1500 used to calculate rate from the number of small squares between R waves at 25 mm/s?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Answer: A. It is the number of 1-mm squares passing in one minute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 347.7 Tm (Explanation: At 25 mm/s, the paper travels 25 x 60 = 1500 mm per minute. Dividing these 1500 small squares by squares) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 334.6 Tm (per beat yields beats per minute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 318.5 Tm (Not B: The distance travelled per cycle varies with RR duration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 305.1 Tm (Not C: Vertical voltage is calibrated at 10 mm per mV.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 291.7 Tm (Not D: A person's actual heart rate is not fixed at 1500 beats per minute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.3 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q44 | 2, 13-14) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 255.1 Tm (44. A patient with massive haemorrhage remains severely hypotensive after blood volume is completely) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 241.4 Tm (restored. Which stage fits?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.7 Tm (Answer: B. Irreversible shock after extensive tissue injury.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 208.4 Tm (Explanation: Severe myocardial, neural and microvascular damage may prevent recovery even after the original) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 195.3 Tm (blood-volume loss has been corrected.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 179.2 Tm (Not A: Compensated shock should retain useful mechanisms for pressure support.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 165.8 Tm (Not C: Postural hypotension should depend on standing and impaired compensation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 152.4 Tm (Not D: An uncomplicated faint generally improves rapidly when lying flat.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.0 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q41 | Circulation 3, slides 25-27) Tj ET
