BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 02 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (45. After a period of low blood volume, aldosterone secretion increases. Which effect is most important over) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (the following hours to days?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: C. Greater renal sodium retention with accompanying water retention.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Aldosterone increases renal Na+ retention, and water follows, helping restore circulating volume over) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (time.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: Sodium and water loss are opposite to aldosterone's action.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not B: Direct pacemaker acceleration is an autonomic effect, not its main role.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: Its principal effect is renal volume conservation, not universal vasodilation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q35 | 12, 29) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (46. A bleeding patient has a nearly normal arterial pressure but cold skin and reduced urine output. What is) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.0 Tm (the best conclusion?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.3 Tm (Answer: B. Low perfusion can coexist with normal pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.0 Tm (Explanation: Sympathetic arteriolar constriction can maintain arterial pressure despite reduced cardiac output and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 545.9 Tm (poor perfusion of some tissues.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.8 Tm (Not A: Shock is defined by inadequate tissue perfusion, not one pressure value.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.4 Tm (Not C: Haemorrhage reduces venous return and usually lowers output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.0 Tm (Not D: Flow can be redistributed away from skin and kidneys.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 489.6 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q18 | Circulation 3, slides 12-13; Chapter 20 ?1) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 466.4 Tm (47. Why can chronic widespread alveolar hypoxia lead to pulmonary hypertension?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.7 Tm (Answer: B. Persistent hypoxic constriction increases lung resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 433.4 Tm (Explanation: When hypoxic constriction affects much of the lung, cumulative resistance rises and pulmonary arterial) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 420.3 Tm (pressure can increase.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.2 Tm (Not A: Recruitment is a different response that lowers resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.8 Tm (Not C: The lecture explicitly associates hypoxia with pulmonary vasoconstriction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.4 Tm (Not D: Lung flow generally continues but faces increased resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 364.0 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q36 | 32) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.8 Tm (48. A sphygmomanometer cuff is held substantially below heart level. How is its pressure reading affected by) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.1 Tm (gravity?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 310.4 Tm (Answer: B. It reads falsely high because of added hydrostatic pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 294.1 Tm (Explanation: A measurement site below the heart has extra hydrostatic pressure, making the reading higher than) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 281.0 Tm (heart-level pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.9 Tm (Not A: Above-heart placement would give a falsely lower reading.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 251.5 Tm (Not C: Arteries do not have the venous valves needed to cancel gravity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 238.1 Tm (Not D: The cuff still estimates arterial pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q62 | 78-80) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 201.5 Tm (49. Once local metabolic dilation restores an organ's blood flow, what prevents dilation from continuing) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 187.8 Tm (unchecked?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 171.1 Tm (Answer: C. The restored flow clears accumulated dilator metabolites.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 154.8 Tm (Explanation: The dilators exist because flow was too low for demand. Once flow matches demand, they are washed away) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 141.7 Tm (as fast as they are made, so the arterioles regain their tone. This negative feedback stops dilation running away.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.6 Tm (Not A: Restored delivery generally improves tissue oxygen availability.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 112.2 Tm (Not B: Sustained lactate accumulation would favour continued dilation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 98.8 Tm (Not D: Vascular smooth muscle remains present and functional.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 85.4 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q10 | 5-6) Tj ET
q .7 .6 .3 RG .8 w 48 54 m 548 54 l S Q
